  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You already know DP on arrays and strings<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;store results in a table, build answers from smaller subproblems. Tree and Graph DP is the same idea, just the&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">structure you traverse changes</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">.</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Instead of moving left to right through an array, you move&nbsp;<strong>through nodes and edges</strong>.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-why-trees-and-graphs-need-dp",
      "title": "1. Why Trees and Graphs Need DP",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Consider a simple question find the&nbsp;<strong>diameter of a tree</strong>&nbsp;(longest path between any two nodes).'<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246579280-975331084.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To find the diameter at node 1, you need to know the longest path going through node 2. To know that, you need results from nodes 4 and 5 first.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You first solve smaller subproblems (children) before solving the bigger problem (parent).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These smaller results are reused to build the final answer.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Since each subproblem is solved only once and reused, no work is repeated.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>That is Dynamic Programming.</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Without DP you'd recompute paths through the same nodes repeatedly.&nbsp;</span>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">With DP compute once at each node, store it, use it at the parent.</span><br>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-tree-dp-nbsp-the-core-idea",
      "title": "2. Tree DP: &nbsp;The Core Idea",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In array DP, you fill a table left to right. In tree DP, you fill results&nbsp;<strong>bottom up</strong>&nbsp;leaves first, root last.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246581106-304990771.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246581106-304990771.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246581106-304990771.png",
          "alt": "Bottom-up tree DP evaluation order: leaves first, root last"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You first compute the leaf nodes (4 and 5) because they don’t depend on anything these are your base cases.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Then you move up to node 2, which uses the results of 4 and 5.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Next, node 3 is processed (it may be a leaf or already ready).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Finally, node 1 is computed using results from nodes 2 and 3.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This follows post-order traversal: process left child, then right child, then the current node using their results.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "What Gets Stored",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In array DP, you store results in&nbsp;<code>dp[i]</code>. In tree DP, you store a result&nbsp;<strong>at each node</strong>&nbsp;either in a map, an array indexed by node, or return values from recursion.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246582653-815934349.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246582653-815934349.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246582653-815934349.png",
          "alt": "Array DP dp[i] versus tree DP result stored per node"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In tree DP, each node itself is a subproblem, so you store a result for every node either in a map, array, or directly return it during recursion.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246584291-681104802.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">So instead of&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">dp[i]</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, you think in terms of&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">dp[node]</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, where each node holds the answer for its subtree.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each node’s value depends on its children, and once computed, it is reused just like in array DP.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The final answer usually comes from the root node or by combining results from all nodes during traversal.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "The General Template",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Almost every tree DP problem follows this skeleton:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "int solve(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return base_case;\n\n    int left  = solve(node.left);    // get left child's result\n    int right = solve(node.right);   // get right child's result\n\n    // combine left and right to get current node's result\n    int result = combine(left, right, node.val);\n\n    // optionally update a global answer\n    globalAnswer = Math.max(globalAnswer, result);\n\n    // return something useful to the parent\n    return something_for_parent;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "<p><strong>Key Insight</strong></p><p>At each node, you compute two different things:&nbsp;<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">one to update the global answer, and one to return to the parent.</span></p>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "Example: Diameter of Tree",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At a node, you get:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "left height from left child",
            "right height from right child"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To update the answer:<br>You take&nbsp;<code>left height + right height</code>&nbsp;→ this gives the longest path passing through this node.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To return to parent:<br>You return&nbsp;<code>1 + max(left height, right height)</code>&nbsp;→ this is the longest single path going upward.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "<hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\">",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-rerooting-technique",
      "title": "3. Rerooting Technique",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Standard tree DP roots the tree at node 0 or node 1 and computes answers relative to that root. But what if the answer depends on which node is the root?",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246585706-710237479.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><b>Problem</b>: for every node, find the sum of distances to all other nodes.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "From node 1: dist to 2 + dist to 3 + dist to 4 = 1+1+2 = 4<br>From node 2: dist to 1 + dist to 3 + dist to 4 = 1+2+1 = 4<br>From node 4: dist to 2 + dist to 1 + dist to 3 = 1+2+3 = 6",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Brute force root at every node and run full DP each time → O(n²).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "<hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "Rerooting: run DP&nbsp;twice",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Pass 1 (top down from root):</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Compute answer assuming current node is root using children's subtree information.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246587189-550563525.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><b>Pass 2 (propagate downward):</b></span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When you move the root from parent to child, update the answer using a simple formula without recomputation.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246588611-410581030.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Moving root from parent P to child C:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246590006-793199084.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before: C was inside P's subtree<br>After: P is now inside C's subtree",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>What changes:</b><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">C loses its own subtree (subtreeSize[C] nodes get closer by 1)</span><br>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">C gains P's remaining tree (n - subtreeSize[C] nodes get farther by 1)</span><br>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "New answer for C = answer for P<br><b>- subtreeSize[C] + (n - subtreeSize[C])</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This computes the answer for every node in O(n) using two passes with no repetition.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-graph-dp",
      "title": "4. Graph DP",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When the structure is a general graph instead of a tree, two things change:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246591426-659483199.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "No natural root: no fixed parent/child direction",
            "Cycles may exist: you can revisit nodes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "5-dag-dp-directed-acyclic-graph",
      "title": "5. DAG DP (Directed Acyclic Graph)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A DAG has no cycles, so we can process nodes in&nbsp;<strong>topological order</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246592870-62701160.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Topological order: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "By the time you process a node, all nodes pointing to it are already processed.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "dp[node] = best result using all incoming paths\n\nFor each node:\n   dp[node] = best of (dp[predecessor] + edge_weight)\n              over all predecessors"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">This is like 1D DP, but on a graph instead of an array.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6-graphs-with-cycles-adding-state",
      "title": "6. Graphs With Cycles: Adding State",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "With cycles, you can loop forever, so you need extra information to control revisits.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "dp[node][visited_mask] = best result at this node\n                         given which nodes are already visited"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The&nbsp;<code>visited_mask</code>&nbsp;is a bitmask where each bit represents whether a node has been visited.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This extra state prevents infinite loops and ensures each state is processed only once.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "Example: Traveling Salesman Problem",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "<b style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Find the shortest route that visits all cities exactly once.</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 1: Define the state</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246594257-418797353.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></b><code>dp[city][mask]</code>&nbsp;= shortest distance to reach&nbsp;<code>city</code>&nbsp;having visited all cities in&nbsp;<code>mask</code>.",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 2: Initialize</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246595890-40510111.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></b>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Start from city 0 with only it visited:</span><br></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "dp[0][0001] = 0",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 3: Pick a current state</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246597424-501996980.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></b>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Take a state&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">(city, mask)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;which is already computed.</span><br></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 4: Try all next moves</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246598838-656882779.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></b>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">From current city, try going to every city that is not yet visited in the mask.</span><br></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 5: Update mask</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246600404-378301773.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></b>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Mark the new city as visited:</span><br></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "newMask = mask | (1 &lt;&lt; nextCity)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 6: Update DP</strong><br>Update the shortest distance:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246601872-665293859.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"><code>dp[nextCity][newMask] = min(existing, dp[city][mask] + distance)</code>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 7: Repeat</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246603352-604956664.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></b>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Do this for all cities and all masks.</span><br></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 8: Final answer</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246604806-295163927.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></b>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">When all cities are visited (mask = all 1s), take the minimum value among all possible ending cities.</span><br></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
